{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by constant warfare with Muslim forces. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab yet he eventually secured his freedom and assumed control over his familial lands in the Levant. Raymond became known for his diplomatic skills and his ability to negotiate truces with Saladin while other crusader leaders often preferred direct military confrontation. He acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and this position allowed Raymond to influence royal policy significantly during a time when the kingdom faced internal division and external threats.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond spent roughly ten years as a prisoner of Nur ad-Din following his capture in 1164 and this experience shaped his worldview regarding the necessity of peace treaties with Muslim neighbors. His release came only after the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos paid a substantial ransom to secure his freedom. This period of imprisonment taught him the value of negotiation over rash military action and he later applied these lessons when governing Tripoli and interacting with the broader crusader states.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count served as regent for the young and sickly King Baldwin IV and he worked tirelessly to maintain stability within the realm despite fierce opposition from the Courtenay faction led by Raynald of Châtillon. Raymond advocated for peaceful coexistence with Saladin while his rivals sought to provoke conflict through raids on Muslim caravans and territories. His political maneuvering helped delay full-scale war for several years although tensions continued to rise between the various noble families within the kingdom.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nRaym